    {
      "id": "the-third-part-of-king-henry-the-sixth-summary-05",
      "text": "Released and restored, Henry delegates government jointly to Warwick and Clarence. He predicts greatness for the young Earl of Richmond, whom Somerset decides to send to Brittany for safety. Edward returns with Burgundian help, gains entry to York by claiming only his dukedom, and then openly reasserts his kingship. Before Henry's supporters can combine their armies, Edward captures Henry again in London. At Coventry, Clarence repudiates Warwick and rejoins his brothers. Edward's victory at Barnet brings Warwick's death, with Montague's death reported to him as he lies wounded. Margaret nevertheless rallies her newly arrived army to continue the war.",
