    {
      "act": 2,
      "sectionId": "the-third-part-of-king-henry-the-sixth-act-44163",
      "label": "ACT II",
      "summary": "Edward and Richard interpret three merging suns as a hopeful sign, then learn of York's murder. Warwick reports his own defeat at Saint Albans and joins them in promoting Edward's claim. Before York, Henry grieves over the display of his enemy's head while the rival forces exchange accusations and threats. At Towton, early reverses provoke renewed vows of revenge; Richard fights Clifford, who escapes when Warwick intervenes. Away from the armies, Henry longs for a shepherd's peace and mourns with men who have unknowingly killed a father and a son. Margaret urges him to flee as the Yorkists win. Clifford dies wounded, and the victors order his head displayed in York's place. Warwick proposes Edward's coronation and marriage to Bona; Richard and George receive the titles Gloucester and Clarence.",
