    {
      "act": 4,
      "sectionId": "the-third-part-of-king-henry-the-sixth-act-123628",
      "label": "ACT IV",
      "summary": "Edward defends his marriage against objections about France, Warwick, and favors to Elizabeth's family. Clarence leaves to join Warwick and seek his daughter; Richard stays with Edward for his own ambitions. Warwick captures Edward in a night attack, prompting pregnant Elizabeth to seek sanctuary. Richard and Hastings free Edward while he hunts under light guard, and he escapes abroad. Henry is restored but entrusts government to Warwick and Clarence. His prediction about young Richmond prompts Somerset to arrange the boy's removal to Brittany. Edward returns with support from Burgundy, wins entry to York by claiming only his dukedom, then accepts renewed proclamation as king. Warwick disperses allies to recruit forces, leaving Henry in London, where Edward captures him before those forces can unite.",
